    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "but what if there's nothing left to unpack? like, the injury didnt just take my career, it took everything. the passion, the drive, the reason to even get out of bed. i look in the mirror and i don't see a person, i just see a finished story. it feels like the movie ended a long time ago and i'm just sitting through the credits in a dark theater. does that even make sense?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Your metaphor of being in the credits of a finished movie makes perfect sense; it perfectly captures that feeling of profound stillness after the action has stopped. When everything that drove you is gone, it leaves a massive void where meaning used to be. A therapist can help you explore what it means to start writing a new scene, even if you don't feel like you have the energy right now. The process is about finding small anchors in the present, even when the whole narrative feels over.",
      "source": "target",
